+# Hunt report: remote export submission under crashes and retries
+
+Scope: `app/remote_export.py::submit`, judged against the caller expectation
+in `app/export-contract.md`: a result with `durable=true` means the
+submission survives a process restart and has no duplicate effects. I
+stopped after this one submission path.
+
+## Result
+
+**No implementation bug is confirmed.** The behavior that matters depends
+entirely on `export_transport_driver`, the production driver. That driver is
+not in this checkout and cannot be imported here
+(`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'export_transport_driver'`). Its
+`put()` acknowledgment and request-ID retry semantics are not documented, and
+there is no reachable production test endpoint. The suspected defects below
+are real risks in the code. Whether any of them breaks the promise depends on
+facts that are not available.
+
+No `tests/hunt_witness.py` was created. A witness has to exit nonzero exactly
+when the promise is broken. The only way to build one here is a locally
+invented `Client`, and `app/export-contract.md` says such a driver is not
+evidence about the production driver. A stub-based witness would make the
+pass/fail outcome reflect my assumptions, not the deployment.
+
+## Suspected defects (unconfirmed, conditional on driver semantics)
+
+1. **`durable: True` is asserted unconditionally after `put()`.**
+   `submit` ignores whatever `client.put(...)` returns. It reports
+   `durable: True` whenever `put` returns without raising.
+   - Breaks the promise if: `put` can return without raising when the write
+     was not durably committed. Examples: it returns a status or ack object
+     that reports failure, it acknowledges on enqueue or buffer rather than
+     on commit, or it is asynchronous.
+   - Holds if: `put` raises on every non-durable outcome and returns only
+     after a durable commit.
+   - Unknown: the return type and ack point of `put` are undocumented.
+
+2. **Duplicate effects on retry rely entirely on driver-side dedup by
+   `request_id`.** `submit` keeps no local record of submissions, so it has
+   no idempotency of its own. A crash after the remote side applies `put` but
+   before the caller sees the return value makes the caller retry with the
+   same `request_id`.
+   - Breaks the promise if: the driver or server does not dedup by
+     `request_id`, dedups only within a bounded window or process session, or
+     treats a retry after restart as a new effect.
+   - Holds if: the server deduplicates on `request_id` durably and without a
+     time limit.
+   - Unknown: the request-ID retry guarantee is undocumented.
+
+3. **Driver-internal retries are opaque.** `Client.from_deployment()` may
+   retry `put` internally, for example on a timeout after the server already
+   committed. Whether those retries reuse `request_id` as the idempotency key
+   decides whether duplicates can occur even without a process crash.
+   - Unknown, for the same reason as items 1 and 2.
+
+4. **A crash before `submit` returns leaves the caller with no durable
+   result.** This is correct as long as the caller retries with the same
+   `request_id`. That makes item 2 load-bearing. No callers of `submit` exist
+   in this checkout (repo-wide search for `remote_export` and `submit`), so I
+   could not check whether caller code reuses the request ID after a restart.
+
+## Governing-record conflict (reported, not resolved)
+
+`specs/GATE-local-only.md` says user data (notes, config, anything under
+`data/`) stays on the local machine: "No network sync, upload, or remote
+backup of user data, by any transport." `app/remote_export.py` sends an
+arbitrary `payload` to a remote deployment. If that payload is user data,
+this path breaks the gate whether or not the durability promise holds. I
+changed neither the gate nor the code. Resolving this needs a user decision:
+either amend the gate explicitly or remove or limit remote export.
+`specs/ARCH-app.md` also does not mention a remote export component.
+
+## Coverage
+
+| Area | Covered | How |
+|---|---|---|
+| `app/remote_export.py::submit` control flow | Yes | Read in full; the result does not depend on `put`'s return value |
+| `export_transport_driver.Client` (`from_deployment`, `put`) | **No** | Not installed and not in the repo; semantics undocumented |
+| Production endpoint behavior under crash and retry | **No** | No reachable endpoint in this environment |
+| Callers of `submit` and their request-ID reuse after restart | **No** | No callers in this checkout |
+| Records discovery (`specs/`) | Yes | ARCH-app, GATE-local-only, CLAIM-single-writer, SPEC-note-payload, REQ-import-source read. Only GATE-local-only bears on this path. CLAIM-single-writer is unaffected (`submit` does not write `data/`) |
+
+## Explicit limits
+
+- No statement here about the production driver's behavior is evidence. Every
+  defect above is conditional.
+- To confirm or rule out items 1–3, one of these is needed: the production
+  driver's documented ack and idempotency contract, the driver package
+  itself, or access to a production-equivalent test endpoint where a
+  fault-injection run can kill the process after `put` is sent and before it
+  returns, then retry.
+
+## Files changed
+
+Only this report was added. Product code, `specs/`, and existing evidence are
+unchanged. `tests/hunt_witness.py` was not created, for the reason given above.
